    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 Expansion",
      "html": "<p>Use the distributive law $a(b+c)=ab+ac$ and identities:</p>$$(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2$$$$(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2$$$$(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2$$<h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$(5x+8)^2 = 25x^2 + 2\\cdot 5x\\cdot 8 + 64 = 25x^2 + 80x + 64$.</li><li>$(4x+2)^2 = 16x^2 + 2\\cdot 4x\\cdot 2 + 4 = 16x^2 + 16x + 4$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(5x+8)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 25x^2 + 80x + 64</p><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(4x+2)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 16x^2 + 16x + 4</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Expand $(2x+3)^2$.",
          "a": "$(2x)^2 + 2\\cdot 2x\\cdot 3 + 3^2 = 4x^2 + 12x + 9$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Factorisation of Quadratics",
      "html": "<p>For $x^2 + bx + c$, find two numbers whose sum is $b$ and product is $c$. For $ax^2+bx+c$ with $a\\ne 1$, use the AC method: find factors of $ac$ that add to $b$, split the middle term and factor by grouping.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Two numbers whose sum is $8$ and product is $7$ are $1$ and $7$. So factorisation is $(x+1)(x+7)$.</li><li>Two numbers whose sum is $15$ and product is $56$ are $7$ and $8$. So factorisation is $(x+7)(x+8)$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Factorise $x^2 + 8x + 7$.<br/><b>A:</b> (x+1)(x+7)</p><p><b>Q:</b> Factorise $x^2 + 15x + 56$.<br/><b>A:</b> (x+7)(x+8)</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Factorise $x^2 + 7x + 12$.",
          "a": "$3+4=7$, $3\\cdot 4=12$: $(x+3)(x+4)$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Factorise $2x^2 + 7x + 3$.",
          "a": "$ac=6$; factors of 6 summing to 7 are 6 and 1. Split: $2x^2 + 6x + x + 3 = 2x(x+3)+1(x+3) = (2x+1)(x+3)$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Linear Equations & Word Problems",
      "html": "<p>Isolate the variable using inverse operations. In word problems, define a variable, translate the words into an equation, solve, and check.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$(5x+8)^2 = 25x^2 + 2\\cdot 5x\\cdot 8 + 64 = 25x^2 + 80x + 64$.</li><li>$(4x+2)^2 = 16x^2 + 2\\cdot 4x\\cdot 2 + 4 = 16x^2 + 16x + 4$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(5x+8)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 25x^2 + 80x + 64</p><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(4x+2)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 16x^2 + 16x + 4</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $3(x-2) = 15$.",
          "a": "$x-2 = 5 \\Rightarrow x = 7$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 4 \u00b7 Change of Subject & Substitution",
      "html": "<p>Treat the target letter as the unknown; use the same rules as solving equations to isolate it.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Divide both sides by $\\pi r^2$.</li><li>Isolate the $\\tfrac12 at^2$ term, then multiply by $2/t^2$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Make $h$ the subject of $V = \\pi r^2 h$.<br/><b>A:</b> h = \\dfrac{V}{\\pi r^2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Given $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$, make $a$ the subject.<br/><b>A:</b> a = \\dfrac{2(s-ut)}{t^2}</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Make $h$ the subject of $V = \\pi r^2 h$. \u2014 h = \\dfrac{V}{\\pi r^2}.</li><li>Given $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$, make $a$ the subject. \u2014 a = \\dfrac{2(s-ut)}{t^2}.</li><li>If $v = u + at$, make $t$ the subject. \u2014 t = \\dfrac{v-u}{a}.</li><li>Make $r$ the subject of the formula: $A = \\pi r^2$. \u2014 $r = \\sqrt{\\frac{A}{\\pi}}$.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Make $r$ the subject of $A = \\pi r^2$.",
          "a": "$r^2 = A/\\pi \\Rightarrow r = \\sqrt{A/\\pi}$."
        },
        {
          "q": "If $v = u + at$, find $t$.",
          "a": "$t = (v-u)/a$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Expansion of algebraic expressions",
      "html": "<p><b>Expansion of algebraic expressions</b> is a key part of Algebraic Processes (SSS 1, Mathematics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Learn the meaning of expansion of algebraic expressions and the terms used with it.</li><li>Know the main types, features or steps involved in expansion of algebraic expressions.</li><li>Be able to give an example of expansion of algebraic expressions and explain why it matters in algebraic processes.</li><li>Practise past questions on expansion of algebraic expressions until you can answer them without notes.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(5x+8)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 25x^2 + 80x + 64 \u2014 $(5x+8)^2 = 25x^2 + 2\\cdot 5x\\cdot 8 + 64 = 25x^2 + 80x + 64$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Expand $(4x+2)^2$.<br/><b>A:</b> 16x^2 + 16x + 4 \u2014 $(4x+2)^2 = 16x^2 + 2\\cdot 4x\\cdot 2 + 4 = 16x^2 + 16x + 4$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Expand $(5x+8)^2$.",
          "a": "25x^2 + 80x + 64 \u2014 $(5x+8)^2 = 25x^2 + 2\\cdot 5x\\cdot 8 + 64 = 25x^2 + 80x + 64$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Expand $(4x+2)^2$.",
          "a": "16x^2 + 16x + 4 \u2014 $(4x+2)^2 = 16x^2 + 2\\cdot 4x\\cdot 2 + 4 = 16x^2 + 16x + 4$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Factorisation of quadratic expressions",
      "html": "<p><b>Factorisation of quadratic expressions</b> is a key part of Algebraic Processes (SSS 1, Mathematics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>So factorisation is $(x+1)(x+7)$.</li><li>So factorisation is $(x+7)(x+8)$.</li><li>Expansion of algebraic expressions is a key part of Algebraic Processes (SSS 1, Mathematics).</li><li>Key points Learn the meaning of expansion of algebraic expressions and the terms used with it.</li><li>Know the main types, features or steps involved in expansion of algebraic expressions.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Factorise $x^2 + 8x + 7$.<br/><b>A:</b> (x+1)(x+7) \u2014 Two numbers whose sum is $8$ and product is $7$ are $1$ and $7$. So factorisation is $(x+1)(x+7)$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Factorise $x^2 + 15x + 56$.<br/><b>A:</b> (x+7)(x+8) \u2014 Two numbers whose sum is $15$ and product is $56$ are $7$ and $8$. So factorisation is $(x+7)(x+8)$.</p>",
